1
00:00:00,804 --> 00:00:05,342
From GitHub Copilot to Microsoft Foundry, Container Apps and AKS.

2
00:00:05,342 --> 00:00:09,252
We ran one issue all the way to production, for real, on

3
00:00:09,252 --> 00:00:13,092
GitHub and Azure, and recorded it from start to finish.

4
00:00:15,171 --> 00:00:18,450
Here is the whole architecture on one page.

5
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:22,035
A developer creates an issue in the repository.

6
00:00:22,286 --> 00:00:26,606
The Copilot cloud agent turns that issue into a pull request.

7
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:29,296
GitHub Actions verifies the code, the

8
00:00:29,296 --> 00:00:32,260
container and the infrastructure in parallel.

9
00:00:32,510 --> 00:00:35,924
To deploy, it signs in to Microsoft

10
00:00:35,924 --> 00:00:40,118
Entra ID with OIDC, with no stored secrets.

11
00:00:40,369 --> 00:00:43,177
The image goes to Azure Container Registry,

12
00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:47,558
and Container Apps and AKS run the same image.

13
00:00:47,811 --> 00:00:52,563
The model is gpt-6.1-sol on Foundry, and the app calls it with

14
00:00:52,563 --> 00:00:57,315
a Managed Identity or a Workload Identity, without an API key.

15
00:00:59,578 --> 00:01:00,346
Demo one.

16
00:01:00,346 --> 00:01:01,626
GitHub Copilot.

17
00:01:01,626 --> 00:01:07,258
Hand the issue to an AI teammate, and a human verifies the result.

18
00:01:09,011 --> 00:01:14,247
Every file that tells Copilot about the team's rules lives in the repository.

19
00:01:14,247 --> 00:01:16,967
First, the repository-wide instructions.

20
00:01:16,967 --> 00:01:20,843
They are attached to every Copilot request automatically.

21
00:01:21,197 --> 00:01:24,806
Path-specific instructions target files with globs, so

22
00:01:24,806 --> 00:01:28,349
Python, Bicep and workflows each get different rules.

23
00:01:28,903 --> 00:01:33,763
Slash prompts and dedicated agents live in the same repository.

24
00:01:33,763 --> 00:01:37,543
Even a read-only reviewer agent is just one file.

25
00:01:38,068 --> 00:01:41,985
The issue form asks for the context, the acceptance criteria and the

26
00:01:41,985 --> 00:01:45,844
constraints, so people write issues that an agent can succeed with.

27
00:01:47,572 --> 00:01:49,652
Issues are created by a script.

28
00:01:49,652 --> 00:01:52,135
Run it as is and it only previews the

29
00:01:52,135 --> 00:01:55,156
issues and the commands, and changes nothing.

30
00:01:55,509 --> 00:01:58,655
With the apply and assign-copilot options, it

31
00:01:58,655 --> 00:02:01,941
creates two issues and assigns them to Copilot.

32
00:02:02,322 --> 00:02:05,865
On the issue page, Copilot appears as the assignee, and a

33
00:02:05,865 --> 00:02:09,906
moment later the timeline records that the agent started working.

34
00:02:15,126 --> 00:02:18,756
The agent opened a draft pull request in about five minutes.

35
00:02:18,756 --> 00:02:21,116
It is linked to the issue, and the plan

36
00:02:21,116 --> 00:02:23,718
and the implementation are kept as commits.

37
00:02:24,073 --> 00:02:27,121
The change is in the UI file and in the tests.

38
00:02:27,121 --> 00:02:31,561
The agent did not just implement the feature; it brought tests too.

39
00:02:32,191 --> 00:02:37,185
The agent's work is kept in its session, about four and a half minutes of it.

40
00:02:37,185 --> 00:02:41,207
You can see the unit tests, a secret scan and the CodeQL scan.

41
00:02:41,207 --> 00:02:43,672
It also tried a browser check, and its

42
00:02:43,672 --> 00:02:46,591
summary says that one could not be completed.

43
00:02:47,147 --> 00:02:50,038
One thing to know: workflows on an agent's pull

44
00:02:50,038 --> 00:02:52,990
request do not run until a person approves them.

45
00:02:52,990 --> 00:02:55,451
We look at that state in the next scene.

46
00:02:57,141 --> 00:03:00,292
In the run list, CI, CodeQL and the dependency

47
00:03:00,292 --> 00:03:03,717
check of the agent's pull request are all waiting.

48
00:03:03,717 --> 00:03:06,525
They are waiting for a person's approval.

49
00:03:06,877 --> 00:03:11,351
On the web, it is one click on Approve and run workflows.

50
00:03:11,351 --> 00:03:15,589
Here, we dispatch the same CI directly on this branch.

51
00:03:16,003 --> 00:03:19,220
Four jobs run in parallel and all of them pass.

52
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:22,026
Code written by an agent goes through the

53
00:03:22,026 --> 00:03:24,763
same checks as code written by a person.

54
00:03:26,603 --> 00:03:31,246
Verification is done, so I switch the draft to Ready for review.

55
00:03:31,246 --> 00:03:33,423
The agent cannot do this step.

56
00:03:33,423 --> 00:03:36,107
Then I ask Copilot for a code review.

57
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:37,968
The review is posted.

58
00:03:37,968 --> 00:03:42,493
It recommends approval and found no open issues in this change.

59
00:03:42,493 --> 00:03:46,156
But a Copilot review does not count as an approval.

60
00:03:46,508 --> 00:03:51,009
A plain merge is blocked, because the ruleset on main prohibits it.

61
00:03:51,009 --> 00:03:55,913
This is a one-person repository, so I merge with an administrator bypass.

62
00:03:55,913 --> 00:04:00,212
The checks have still run, and the decision stays with a person.

63
00:04:00,565 --> 00:04:04,521
The pull request is merged and the linked issue is closed.

64
00:04:04,521 --> 00:04:07,794
From issue to pull request, verification, review

65
00:04:07,794 --> 00:04:11,341
and merge, a human stayed in control the whole time.

66
00:04:17,011 --> 00:04:17,721
Demo two.

67
00:04:17,721 --> 00:04:19,457
The GitHub repository.

68
00:04:19,457 --> 00:04:24,979
Protection rules and security checks are defined as files and scripts.

69
00:04:26,145 --> 00:04:29,168
Repository protection is applied by one script.

70
00:04:29,168 --> 00:04:34,185
Run it as is and it changes nothing; it only previews the calls it would make.

71
00:04:34,539 --> 00:04:37,650
Add the apply option and it really applies them.

72
00:04:37,650 --> 00:04:41,734
Security features, labels, the deployment approval environment,

73
00:04:41,734 --> 00:04:46,011
the ruleset and the merge options turn to done, one after another.

74
00:04:48,517 --> 00:04:52,805
Checking the applied ruleset through the API: protection on main is

75
00:04:52,805 --> 00:04:56,837
active, and it contains deletion blocking, force-push blocking,

76
00:04:56,837 --> 00:05:01,573
required pull requests, required checks, code scanning and Copilot review.

77
00:05:01,925 --> 00:05:04,469
Settings are code, so you can run the script

78
00:05:04,469 --> 00:05:07,013
any number of times and get the same result.

79
00:05:08,501 --> 00:05:13,049
A new contributor who opens the repo in Codespaces gets an environment

80
00:05:13,049 --> 00:05:18,053
with Python and the Azure CLI, exactly as this dev container file defines it.

81
00:05:19,472 --> 00:05:22,577
Let's run the same commands in a terminal.

82
00:05:22,577 --> 00:05:24,056
All unit tests pass.

83
00:05:27,666 --> 00:05:31,181
Start the app in mock mode and it runs without Azure.

84
00:05:31,181 --> 00:05:34,232
healthz is open without credentials, and every

85
00:05:34,232 --> 00:05:37,482
other route returns 401 when called without them.

86
00:05:39,470 --> 00:05:43,058
Now we open a pull request that is risky on purpose.

87
00:05:43,058 --> 00:05:46,370
It contains vulnerable Python code, a vulnerable

88
00:05:46,370 --> 00:05:49,958
dependency and a workflow that uses untrusted input.

89
00:05:54,257 --> 00:05:57,114
As soon as the pull request opens, CodeQL,

90
00:05:57,114 --> 00:06:01,128
dependency review and the Copilot review run automatically.

91
00:06:01,128 --> 00:06:03,713
In the checks list, three have failed.

92
00:06:04,066 --> 00:06:07,385
CodeQL reports alerts for command injection, path

93
00:06:07,385 --> 00:06:11,314
traversal, debug mode, and code injection in the workflow.

94
00:06:14,007 --> 00:06:16,392
The GitHub page shows the same state.

95
00:06:16,392 --> 00:06:19,938
The merge status panel lists the failed checks and says

96
00:06:19,938 --> 00:06:23,871
that the security alerts CodeQL found are blocking the merge.

97
00:06:26,968 --> 00:06:29,235
Under Security, code scanning lists the

98
00:06:29,235 --> 00:06:32,200
alerts from this pull request, with their severity.

99
00:06:32,671 --> 00:06:34,824
We will not merge this pull request.

100
00:06:34,824 --> 00:06:37,396
We confirmed that the checks stopped it, as

101
00:06:37,396 --> 00:06:40,207
intended, so we close it and delete the branch.

102
00:06:42,094 --> 00:06:45,274
Dependency updates are handled by Dependabot.

103
00:06:45,274 --> 00:06:48,100
One file tells it to check actions, pip,

104
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:50,926
Docker and the dev container every week.

105
00:06:52,177 --> 00:06:54,241
The checks are already running.

106
00:06:54,241 --> 00:06:58,369
If nothing is outdated, no pull request is the correct result.

107
00:07:00,323 --> 00:07:03,448
Now we check on GitHub what the script applied.

108
00:07:03,448 --> 00:07:08,435
The ruleset main-protection is active, and it targets the main branch only.

109
00:07:08,826 --> 00:07:12,958
The rule list has deletion blocking, force-push blocking, required

110
00:07:12,958 --> 00:07:17,466
pull requests, required status checks, code scanning and Copilot review.

111
00:07:19,161 --> 00:07:22,357
The deployment environment azure-demo has a protection

112
00:07:22,357 --> 00:07:26,145
rule, so a deployment starts only after a named person approves.

113
00:07:27,402 --> 00:07:29,274
Security features are on too.

114
00:07:29,274 --> 00:07:33,407
The dependency graph, Dependabot alerts and security updates are

115
00:07:33,407 --> 00:07:38,250
enabled, and version updates are handled by the dependabot.yml we just saw.

116
00:07:40,502 --> 00:07:41,527
Demo three.

117
00:07:41,527 --> 00:07:42,925
GitHub Actions.

118
00:07:42,925 --> 00:07:48,422
Parallel verification, and OIDC deployment without secrets.

119
00:07:49,663 --> 00:07:52,769
When you open a pull request, CI runs four jobs at

120
00:07:52,769 --> 00:07:56,000
once: unit tests, container build with a smoke test,

121
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,479
infrastructure validation, and workflow and script lint.

122
00:08:00,134 --> 00:08:04,970
Including CodeQL and the dependency check, all eight checks passed.

123
00:08:04,970 --> 00:08:09,518
These job names are exactly the required checks in the ruleset.

124
00:08:09,871 --> 00:08:13,127
The container smoke test runs fourteen checks, on a

125
00:08:13,127 --> 00:08:16,447
read-only file system with every capability dropped.

126
00:08:18,003 --> 00:08:21,303
The trust used for deployment is created by one script.

127
00:08:21,303 --> 00:08:24,363
The preview only shows the managed identity and the

128
00:08:24,363 --> 00:08:27,723
roles it would create, and the variables it would store.

129
00:08:28,077 --> 00:08:31,203
With apply, it creates the managed identity and the

130
00:08:31,203 --> 00:08:35,372
federated credential, and grants roles only on the target resources.

131
00:08:35,372 --> 00:08:38,989
The script asks GitHub for the exact subject format of this

132
00:08:38,989 --> 00:08:43,341
repository, because recent repositories put numeric IDs in the subject.

133
00:08:43,697 --> 00:08:48,482
There are zero secrets, in the repository and in the environment.

134
00:08:48,482 --> 00:08:51,353
The only stored values are identifiers.

135
00:08:52,141 --> 00:08:57,130
The trust is narrowed to one environment of this repository, azure-demo, and

136
00:08:57,130 --> 00:09:02,053
the roles exist only on the resource group, the registry and the namespace.

137
00:09:09,990 --> 00:09:11,780
The GitHub page agrees.

138
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:16,372
There are no environment secrets and no repository secrets.

139
00:09:16,372 --> 00:09:20,574
The workflow only uses the token it gets through OIDC.

140
00:09:22,390 --> 00:09:26,866
When we run the deploy workflow, it stops at the environment approval.

141
00:09:26,866 --> 00:09:31,534
The status is waiting, and until someone approves, nothing touches Azure.

142
00:09:31,887 --> 00:09:35,851
After the approval, it signs in to Azure with OIDC and no

143
00:09:35,851 --> 00:09:40,719
secrets, builds and pushes the image, and then deploys a new revision.

144
00:09:59,250 --> 00:10:02,143
When the deployment finishes, the app's info

145
00:10:02,143 --> 00:10:05,233
endpoint returns the version of the new commit.

146
00:10:05,233 --> 00:10:07,074
That value is the image tag.

147
00:10:07,426 --> 00:10:09,772
We also verify the build provenance.

148
00:10:09,772 --> 00:10:12,509
It checks the signed attestation that this

149
00:10:12,509 --> 00:10:15,898
image was built by this workflow in this repository.

150
00:10:24,086 --> 00:10:27,036
In the Azure portal, here is the federated

151
00:10:27,036 --> 00:10:30,828
credential of the managed identity for GitHub Actions.

152
00:10:30,828 --> 00:10:35,462
There is one credential, and its issuer is GitHub's token service.

153
00:10:41,539 --> 00:10:45,358
Open it and the organization and the repository carry numeric

154
00:10:45,358 --> 00:10:49,490
IDs, and the trusted subject is the single environment azure-demo.

155
00:10:49,490 --> 00:10:51,932
The portal recommends this immutable ID

156
00:10:51,932 --> 00:10:54,811
format, and the script uses it from the start.

157
00:11:01,233 --> 00:11:05,051
The roles given to this managed identity exist only on the

158
00:11:05,051 --> 00:11:08,935
target resources: Contributor on the container app resource

159
00:11:08,935 --> 00:11:12,753
group, AcrPush on the registry, and the AKS cluster roles.

160
00:11:14,940 --> 00:11:15,838
Demo four.

161
00:11:15,838 --> 00:11:17,454
Microsoft Foundry.

162
00:11:17,454 --> 00:11:21,852
The same code calls the model without an API key.

163
00:11:23,035 --> 00:11:26,471
For this demo we chose gpt-6.1-sol as the model.

164
00:11:26,471 --> 00:11:30,766
One script creates the Foundry resource and the project, and

165
00:11:30,766 --> 00:11:35,419
deploys the model, so we simply run it with its default settings.

166
00:11:35,771 --> 00:11:39,971
The Foundry resource and the model are deployed in under two minutes.

167
00:11:40,323 --> 00:11:43,986
The deployment list shows gpt-6.1-sol, and the

168
00:11:43,986 --> 00:11:49,003
connection file has only a resource name and a deployment name.

169
00:11:49,003 --> 00:11:50,595
There is no API key.

170
00:11:54,902 --> 00:11:56,303
We run the app locally.

171
00:11:56,303 --> 00:11:59,592
There is no key in the code; it calls Foundry with the

172
00:11:59,592 --> 00:12:02,942
token of the account I signed in with through az login.

173
00:12:04,046 --> 00:12:08,545
Ask a question in the browser and gpt-6.1-sol answers.

174
00:12:08,545 --> 00:12:12,878
Effort is high, so it takes a while to think deeply.

175
00:12:21,509 --> 00:12:24,642
Below the answer you see the instance and version that

176
00:12:24,642 --> 00:12:28,762
responded, and the exchange is recorded in the recent conversation too.

177
00:12:28,762 --> 00:12:31,373
The Copilot agent built that feature earlier.

178
00:12:32,839 --> 00:12:36,590
The info endpoint reports backend foundry,

179
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:41,055
model gpt-6.1-sol, auth entra-id and effort xhigh.

180
00:12:41,055 --> 00:12:46,591
GPT has no max level, so the app sends the highest one, xhigh.

181
00:12:46,944 --> 00:12:49,548
We sent the same question to a server

182
00:12:49,548 --> 00:12:53,066
started with effort low and to the default server.

183
00:12:53,066 --> 00:12:58,273
In this recording, low took about eight seconds and xhigh about seventeen.

184
00:12:58,273 --> 00:13:02,424
The code is the same; only an environment variable differs.

185
00:13:09,382 --> 00:13:12,942
This is the Foundry resource in the Azure portal.

186
00:13:12,942 --> 00:13:17,374
Its kind is AIServices, and it was created in Sweden Central.

187
00:13:23,551 --> 00:13:27,652
The roles of the Container Apps app's managed identity are just

188
00:13:27,652 --> 00:13:32,731
Cognitive Services User on the Foundry account and AcrPull for pulling images.

189
00:13:32,731 --> 00:13:36,247
This role, not an API key, is what allows model calls.

190
00:13:37,944 --> 00:13:41,203
This is the project home in the Foundry portal.

191
00:13:41,203 --> 00:13:46,056
The app calls the model only with an Entra ID token, never with a key.

192
00:13:51,014 --> 00:13:54,440
The models page has the deployment gpt-6.1-sol.

193
00:13:54,440 --> 00:13:58,959
It is a Global Standard deployment and its state is succeeded.

194
00:13:58,959 --> 00:14:01,656
The name that the app's FOUNDRY_MODEL

195
00:14:01,656 --> 00:14:04,718
points to is exactly this deployment name.

196
00:14:06,969 --> 00:14:07,851
Demo five.

197
00:14:07,851 --> 00:14:09,704
Azure Container Apps.

198
00:14:09,704 --> 00:14:13,497
Scale-to-zero and revision canary releases.

199
00:14:14,682 --> 00:14:17,255
Deploying to Container Apps is one script.

200
00:14:17,255 --> 00:14:20,379
It creates the registry and the environment, builds

201
00:14:20,379 --> 00:14:23,380
the image in the registry, deploys the app, waits

202
00:14:23,380 --> 00:14:26,994
until it responds, and then shows the address and the info.

203
00:14:31,737 --> 00:14:36,669
We sent no request after the deployment, so there are zero replicas.

204
00:14:36,669 --> 00:14:38,265
No compute is running.

205
00:14:45,316 --> 00:14:47,265
The first request took twenty-six

206
00:14:47,265 --> 00:14:49,924
seconds, because a replica had to be started.

207
00:14:50,850 --> 00:14:57,042
The second request finished in 0.03 seconds, and the replica count is now one.

208
00:15:10,565 --> 00:15:13,472
The same smoke test passes all fourteen

209
00:15:13,472 --> 00:15:16,901
checks against the deployed HTTPS address too.

210
00:15:18,375 --> 00:15:20,425
New versions ship as revisions.

211
00:15:20,425 --> 00:15:24,325
Status shows the current split, and deploy v2 creates a new

212
00:15:24,325 --> 00:15:28,887
revision with zero percent of the traffic, so users are not affected.

213
00:15:34,741 --> 00:15:38,922
Canary 10 sends only ten percent of the traffic to the new version.

214
00:15:38,922 --> 00:15:42,229
We look at this state in a browser in the next scene.

215
00:15:42,582 --> 00:15:46,669
If all is well, promote switches to one hundred percent.

216
00:15:46,669 --> 00:15:49,734
Thirty probe calls are all answered by v2.

217
00:15:53,057 --> 00:15:55,107
If there is a problem, roll back.

218
00:15:55,107 --> 00:15:58,647
It is not a redeploy; only the traffic weights change, so

219
00:15:58,647 --> 00:16:02,249
the previous revision gets one hundred percent right away.

220
00:16:05,339 --> 00:16:09,416
Here is the app in a browser while the canary is at ten percent.

221
00:16:09,416 --> 00:16:12,475
The chips at the top show the platform, aca, the

222
00:16:12,475 --> 00:16:16,043
replica that answered, the revision and the app version.

223
00:16:16,504 --> 00:16:20,152
Show traffic distribution sends twenty requests and draws

224
00:16:20,152 --> 00:16:24,184
the answers per revision, per instance and per version as bars.

225
00:16:24,184 --> 00:16:28,216
The new version is receiving only a small share of the traffic.

226
00:16:33,172 --> 00:16:37,147
The top terminal watches the replica count every four seconds, and the

227
00:16:37,147 --> 00:16:41,236
bottom one applies load for two minutes with twenty concurrent requests.

228
00:16:45,851 --> 00:16:50,035
The mock answer takes about a second, so requests pile up.

229
00:16:50,035 --> 00:16:53,282
When the concurrency passes five per replica,

230
00:16:53,282 --> 00:16:57,683
replicas are added: one, three, and then the maximum of five.

231
00:17:17,554 --> 00:17:21,044
At the end, about nineteen hundred requests all

232
00:17:21,044 --> 00:17:24,981
returned HTTP 200, with a median of about one second.

233
00:17:24,981 --> 00:17:30,106
Once the load is gone, the replicas shrink again after a few minutes.

234
00:17:31,678 --> 00:17:34,288
The Azure portal shows the same result.

235
00:17:34,288 --> 00:17:38,571
The resource group holds the container app, the app environment,

236
00:17:38,571 --> 00:17:43,054
the registry, the managed identities and a Log Analytics workspace.

237
00:17:47,481 --> 00:17:49,141
This is the revisions page.

238
00:17:49,141 --> 00:17:52,830
There is one active revision with one hundred percent of the

239
00:17:52,830 --> 00:17:57,010
traffic, and it has zero replicas now because there are no requests.

240
00:17:57,010 --> 00:18:00,207
The Inactive tab keeps the earlier revisions, so you

241
00:18:00,207 --> 00:18:03,465
can bring one back with zero percent traffic anytime.

242
00:18:08,315 --> 00:18:11,288
The Scale page has a minimum of zero and a

243
00:18:11,288 --> 00:18:14,332
maximum of five replicas with an HTTP rule.

244
00:18:14,332 --> 00:18:18,083
Scale-to-zero and scale-out are exactly this setting.

245
00:18:20,337 --> 00:18:21,255
Demo six.

246
00:18:21,255 --> 00:18:22,071
AKS.

247
00:18:22,071 --> 00:18:27,273
Rolling updates, autoscaling and Workload Identity.

248
00:18:28,436 --> 00:18:31,621
An AKS deployment takes five to ten minutes.

249
00:18:31,621 --> 00:18:35,459
It creates the cluster, the registry and the Workload

250
00:18:35,459 --> 00:18:39,296
Identity, pushes the image and applies the manifests.

251
00:18:39,296 --> 00:18:41,324
The waiting part is sped up.

252
00:18:47,073 --> 00:18:49,652
On one node you see the deployment, the

253
00:18:49,652 --> 00:18:52,761
HPA, the PDB, the service and the pods at once.

254
00:18:55,708 --> 00:18:58,097
Connect with port forwarding and run the

255
00:18:58,097 --> 00:19:00,844
smoke test, and the same fourteen checks pass.

256
00:19:08,401 --> 00:19:11,461
The top pane watches the HPA and the pods.

257
00:19:11,461 --> 00:19:14,449
The kubectl watch option accepts only one

258
00:19:14,449 --> 00:19:18,529
resource type, so we used the watch command to see both.

259
00:19:18,885 --> 00:19:22,140
Start the load generator in the bottom pane, and CPU

260
00:19:22,140 --> 00:19:26,397
utilization passes the target, so the pods grow from two up to five.

261
00:19:32,403 --> 00:19:37,275
Turn the load off and, after the stabilization window, the pods shrink again.

262
00:19:50,592 --> 00:19:53,090
The v2 image was pushed beforehand.

263
00:19:53,090 --> 00:19:57,301
When we change the image to v2, a new pod is created first,

264
00:19:57,301 --> 00:20:01,512
and the old pod is removed only after the new one is ready.

265
00:20:07,279 --> 00:20:11,191
The rollout succeeds, and the history keeps the revisions.

266
00:20:18,199 --> 00:20:22,879
If something is wrong, a single rollout undo brings the old version back.

267
00:20:39,802 --> 00:20:42,084
The service account's annotation points

268
00:20:42,084 --> 00:20:44,482
to the client ID of the managed identity.

269
00:20:47,886 --> 00:20:51,884
Only four values are injected into the pod: the client

270
00:20:51,884 --> 00:20:56,104
ID, the tenant ID, the token file and the authority host.

271
00:20:56,104 --> 00:20:57,510
There is no secret.

272
00:20:58,257 --> 00:21:00,889
If we try to create a privileged pod, the

273
00:21:00,889 --> 00:21:03,585
Pod Security restricted policy rejects it.

274
00:21:05,182 --> 00:21:07,944
This is the overview of the AKS cluster.

275
00:21:07,944 --> 00:21:11,743
It is running, the networking is Azure CNI Overlay, and

276
00:21:11,743 --> 00:21:15,886
the container registry that supplies the images is attached.

277
00:21:26,868 --> 00:21:30,403
The node pool autoscales, and its nodes are ready.

278
00:21:30,403 --> 00:21:33,444
When pods increase, nodes can increase too.

279
00:21:36,933 --> 00:21:42,673
In the security configuration, the OIDC issuer and Workload Identity are on.

280
00:21:42,673 --> 00:21:47,205
That is why a pod can get an Entra ID token without secrets.

281
00:21:50,556 --> 00:21:54,244
This is the workloads page of the Kubernetes resources.

282
00:21:54,244 --> 00:21:57,597
Both pods of the cloud-copilot-demo deployment are

283
00:21:57,597 --> 00:22:01,151
ready, and the load generator is scaled down to zero.

284
00:22:01,151 --> 00:22:03,900
It is the same state we saw with kubectl.

285
00:22:06,209 --> 00:22:08,421
Last, the Foundry connection.

286
00:22:08,421 --> 00:22:13,913
The apps on Container Apps and AKS call the same model, with no secrets.

287
00:22:15,068 --> 00:22:18,927
Load the Foundry environment file and run the same deploy script

288
00:22:18,927 --> 00:22:22,725
again, and the app calls the Foundry model instead of the mock.

289
00:22:22,725 --> 00:22:25,076
The image is rebuilt with the new code.

290
00:22:32,754 --> 00:22:35,308
This is the Container Apps page.

291
00:22:35,308 --> 00:22:41,293
The backend is foundry, the model is gpt-6.1-sol, and the auth is entra-id.

292
00:22:41,293 --> 00:22:46,002
The app's Managed Identity makes the call without a secret.

293
00:22:54,114 --> 00:22:55,647
AKS works the same way.

294
00:22:55,647 --> 00:22:58,978
Pushing again with the same tag would not make the

295
00:22:58,978 --> 00:23:02,442
nodes pull the image, so we deployed with a new tag.

296
00:23:17,294 --> 00:23:20,486
The pod calls with its Workload Identity, and

297
00:23:20,486 --> 00:23:24,032
the page shows platform aks and model gpt-6.1-sol.

298
00:23:24,032 --> 00:23:26,940
Between the same image and the same code,

299
00:23:26,940 --> 00:23:29,918
the only difference is who it signs in as.

300
00:23:38,635 --> 00:23:41,364
To wrap up: from issue to pull request,

301
00:23:41,364 --> 00:23:44,724
verification, review and merge, a human stays in

302
00:23:44,724 --> 00:23:49,483
control, and settings and security are defined as files and scripts.

303
00:23:49,835 --> 00:23:53,756
We deployed with OIDC and no stored secrets, and called

304
00:23:53,756 --> 00:23:57,892
the model with a Managed Identity and a Workload Identity.

305
00:23:57,892 --> 00:23:59,675
There is no key anywhere.

306
00:24:00,026 --> 00:24:02,864
Running it also taught us a few things.

307
00:24:02,864 --> 00:24:08,322
An agent's pull request needs a person's approval before its workflows run.

308
00:24:08,322 --> 00:24:12,469
The OIDC subject in new repositories carries numeric IDs.

309
00:24:12,469 --> 00:24:15,890
And kubectl watch takes only one resource type.
